`progress` is measured against `max`, and each platform's widget wants that
expressed in its own units.

GTK's fraction and Compose's progress are both 0..1, so a platform that passes
`value` straight through renders 2-of-10 as a full bar -- in the golden it is
a fifth of one. HTML's <progress> takes both numbers, so it does not divide,
but max="0" is not valid there either.

The shared cause was `max` having no default and rendering as its zero value.
It defaults to 1, which is the value HTML's element already assumes and the
only one that leaves value/max meaning what it says.

deny out/none/html/index.html `max="0"` -- an omitted `max` defaults to 1, and a zero maximum is neither valid HTML nor a divisor the other platforms could use

-- but max="0" is not
valid there either.

The shared cause was `max` having no default and rendering as its zero value.
It defaults to 1, which is the value HTML's element already assumes and the
only one that leaves value/max meaning what it says.

2 of 10 is a fifth of the bar, not all of it.
-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        gtk4(gir="builtin")
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
    none {
        html
    }
}

window(title="t") {
    progress(value=2.0, max=10.0)
    progress(value=0.5)
}
